Apache Kafka Integration and server deployment
4. Deploy Kafka Server Using Docker
4.1 Pull Kafka Image
docker pull apache/kafka:4.1.1

This downloads the Kafka broker image locally.

4.2 Kafka Deployment Using Docker Run
docker run -d \
  --name dev_kafka \
  -p 9092:9092 \
  apache/kafka:4.1.1

Explanation:
-d → Run in background


--name dev_kafka → Assign container name


-p 9092:9092 → Expose Kafka broker port


Kafka starts in KRaft mode by default



4.3 Verify Kafka Server Status
docker ps

Expected output:
dev_kafka   apache/kafka:4.1.1   Up   0.0.0.0:9092->9092/tcp

This confirms the Kafka broker is running.

5. Access Kafka Server Shell
Kafka CLI tools are available inside the container.
docker exec -it dev_kafka /bin/bash

Navigate to Kafka binaries:
cd /opt/kafka/bin


6. Verify Kafka Broker Health
./kafka-broker-api-versions.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

Purpose:
Confirms broker is reachable


Confirms Kafka startup success


Validates network connectivity



7. Kafka Topic Management
7.1 List Existing Topics
./kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --list

Empty output means no topics exist


This is normal for a new Kafka cluster



7.2 Create Kafka Topic
./kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --create \
  --topic dev-topic1 \
  --partitions 3 \
  --replication-factor 1

Explanation:
dev-topic1 → Topic name


3 partitions → Enables parallelism


replication-factor 1 → Single broker setup



7.3 Verify Topic Creation
./kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --list

Expected:
dev-topic1


7.4 Describe Topic Details
./kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --describe \
  --topic dev-topic1

Shows:
Partition count


Leader broker


Replica assignments


In-sync replicas (ISR)



8. Kafka Partition Concepts
Messages are written to partitions


Ordering is guaranteed within a partition


Partitions enable:


Parallel producers


Parallel consumers


One consumer per partition per consumer group



9. Validate Kafka Using CLI (Server-Level Test)
9.1 Start Console Producer
./kafka-console-producer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic dev-topic1

Type messages:
hello kafka


9.2 Start Console Consumer (New Terminal)
./kafka-console-consumer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic dev-topic1 \
  --from-beginning

Expected output:
hello kafka

This confirms:
Broker is working


Topic is functional


Data flow is correct



10. Kafka Server Lifecycle Management
Stop Kafka Server
docker stop dev_kafka


Start Kafka Server
docker start dev_kafka


Restart Kafka Server
docker restart dev_kafka


Remove Kafka Server (Deletes Data)
docker rm -f dev_kafka

⚠️ This removes:
Topics


Messages


Offsets



11. Kafka Server Ports
Port
Purpose
9092
Kafka client communication
9093
Controller communication (internal)


12. Common Server-Level Issues
Kafka Not Starting
Check port availability


Check Docker logs:


docker logs dev_kafka


Connection Refused
Ensure container is running


Verify port mapping


Verify localhost:9092



13. Production Notes
Use Docker volumes for data persistence


Use Docker Compose or Kubernetes for clusters


Increase replication-factor for fault tolerance


Monitor broker health and disk usage


Secure Kafka with SSL/SASL in production

Set up of Kafka Server in the code



spring.application.name=kafka
# ===============================
# Kafka Bootstrap Server
# ===============================
spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers=localhost:9092
# ===============================
# Kafka Producer Configuration
# ===============================
spring.kafka.producer.key-serializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
spring.kafka.producer.value-serializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
# Message delivery guarantees
spring.kafka.producer.acks=all
spring.kafka.producer.retries=3
# ===============================
# Kafka Consumer Configuration
# ===============================
spring.kafka.consumer.group-id=dev-consumer-group
spring.kafka.consumer.auto-offset-reset=earliest
spring.kafka.consumer.key-deserializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.value-deserializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer
# ===============================
# Listener Configuration (Optional but Recommended)
# ===============================
spring.kafka.listener.missing-topics-fatal=false
# ===============================
# H2 Database Configuration
# ===============================
#springdoc.api-docs.enabled=true
#springdoc.swagger-ui.enabled=true
#spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:kafkadb
#spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver
#spring.datasource.username=sa
#spring.datasource.password=
#spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect
#spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
#spring.jpa.show-sql=true
## Enable H2 Console
#spring.h2.console.enabled=true
#spring.h2.console.path=/h2-console
## ===============================
## Kafka Configuration
## ===============================
#spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers=localhost:9092
## Producer
#spring.kafka.producer.key-serializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
#spring.kafka.producer.value-serializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
#spring.kafka.producer.acks=all
#spring.kafka.producer.retries=3
## Consumer
#spring.kafka.consumer.group-id=dev-consumer-group
#spring.kafka.consumer.auto-offset-reset=earliest
#spring.kafka.consumer.key-deserializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer
#spring.kafka.consumer.value-deserializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer
#spring.kafka.listener.missing-topics-fatal=false






package com.dev.kafka.kafka;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;


@Service
public class ProducerService {


   private static final String TOPICNAME="dev-topic1";
   private final KafkaTemplate<String,String> kafkaTemplate;
   public ProducerService(KafkaTemplate<String,String> kafkaTemplate){
       this.kafkaTemplate=kafkaTemplate;
   }
   public void sendMessage(String message){
       kafkaTemplate.send(TOPICNAME,message);
   }
}
package com.dev.kafka.kafka;


import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;












@Service
public class ConsumerService {
   @KafkaListener(
           topics = "dev-topic1",
   groupId="dev-consumer-group"
   )
   public String consumeMessage(String message){
       System.out.println("Consumed message: "+ message);
       return message;
   }
}


